  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery shows extensive cloud cover \nover Upper Michigan in association with a shortwave trough crossing \nnorthern Ontario. This has helped to limit fog formation relative to \nlast night, with overnight temperatures also running a bit warmer in \nthe low 50s across most of the area. The western edge of the high \ncloud cover has cleared the far western UP, allowing some patchy low \nstratus to form in those areas. Skies will gradually clear across \nthe rest of the UP today as the shortwave departs east into Quebec \nand allows upper level high pressure to rebuild over the region. \nHighs will trend slightly upward today into the low 70s across much \nof the area, with the exception of some upper 60s in the Keweenaw \nand locations near Lake Superior. Dewpoints should mix out nicely \nagain this afternoon, allowing minimum RH values to dip into the low \n40s to perhaps the upper 30s in a few interior locations. Do not \nexpect any fire weather concerns given weak pressure gradient and \nlight winds.\n\nHigh pressure will remain over the Great Lakes through the weekend \nas part of an Omega-like blocking pattern, bookended by upper lows \nover the Mid Atlantic and the Canadian prairie provinces. In terms of \nsensible weather this will more or less maintain the status quo \nacross the UP through early next week, yielding continued dry \nweather with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s each afternoon. \nAnother weak shortwave riding the western periphery of the ridge \nthis weekend could bring a few showers to the far western UP late \nSaturday into Sunday, but NBM continues to cap chances for \nmeasurable precipitation around 20% near Ironwood. Will need to keep \nan eye on minimum RH values over the next few days with seasonable \nwarm temps and good afternoon dewpoint mixing, but weak pressure \ngradient and light winds will continue to limit fire weather \nconcerns.\n\nHigh pressure finally breaks down Monday into Tuesday, paving the \nway for more substantive troughing to move into the region during \nthe middle of the week. There is still quite a bit of variability \nwith regards to the details ranging from strength, timing, and \norientation of the trough to the potential for a stronger surface \nlow to lift out of the Plains and track close to the area. Despite \nthe model spread, there is broad agreement on more widespread rain \nreturning to the region Tuesday into Wednesday. NBM currently \ndepicts a 30-40% chance for at least a half inch of rain across much \nof the UP during this time period.",
